  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/otocinclus_vestitus_paraguay_en/",
      "html": "<p>Otocinclus vestitus can be easily identified by the typical pattern in the caudal fin. The species name <em>vestitus</em> is Latin and means \"dressed\".</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/otocinclus_vestitus_paraguay_en/",
      "html": "<p>Otocinclus vestitus has a very large distribution. It is found in Peru, Bolivia, and Paraguay.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/otocinclus_vestitus_paraguay_en/",
      "html": "<p>Keeping O. vestitus is very easy; it is a typical beginner's species. Lovers of well planted aquaria enjoy it very much, as it is a perfect algae eater that feeds on the algae from the top of the leaves without hurting them at all.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/otocinclus_vestitus_paraguay_en/",
      "html": "<p>It feeds on algae from the top of leaves.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/otocinclus_vestitus_paraguay_en/",
      "html": "<p>Breeding is very easy. For breeding a species-specific tank should be used. Technically speaking, breeding is very much like that of Corydoras species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/otocinclus_vestitus_paraguay_en/",
      "html": "<p>Among the O. vestitus received from Paraguay was one very unusual xanthoristic specimen. Xanthorism is a mutation in which the cells with yellow pigment increase. It is a very rare occasion that such a conspicuous animal survives in the wild and grows to adult size. Old trade name: O. arnoldi.</p>"
    }
  ],
